    "case_id": "757",
    "case_summary": "A 3-year-old Chinese boy, first child of healthy non-consanguineous parents, with no family history of kidney disease. Prenatal ultrasound showed slightly enhanced parenchymal echoes and enlarged kidneys bilaterally. Born at term (40 weeks) by cesarean section with normal birth parameters (height 51 cm, weight 3720 g, Apgar 10-10-10). Contracted pneumonia at 2 weeks. At 3 weeks, admitted for shortness of breath and poor appetite. Development: Could raise head at 6 months, turn over at 8 months, sit independently at 12 months. At 3 years, height 86 cm (-3 SD) and weight 10.6 kg (-2.6 SD). Unable to walk independently or speak.\n\nOn admission at 3 weeks: Weight loss (3170 g), dehydration, and hypotonia. Required respiratory support through continuous positive airway pressure. At 4 weeks: Showed hypernatremia and involuntary eye movements. Physical development significantly delayed.\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood biochemistry: Elevated serum creatinine (218.00 μmol/L), high plasma urea (32.78 mmol/L), hyperkalemia (5.7 mmol/L), hyperchloremia (113.6 mmol/L), elevated liver enzymes (AST 85 U/L, ALT 107 U/L, γ-GT 465 U/L)- Blood gas analysis: Anemia (Hb 81 g/L), severe metabolic acidosis (pH=7.0, HCO3-=8.4 mmol/L)- Thyroid function: Elevated TSH (14.86 uIU/mL), slightly low free thyroid hormone (10.49 pmol/L)Imaging Studies:- Renal ultrasound: Increased renal echogenicity- Cardiac ultrasound: Patent foramen ovale- Intracranial MRI: No definite structural abnormalities- Abdominal MRI: Dilatation of intrahepatic bile ducts in left liver lobe (3.6 mm)- Digital radiography of knees: No definite structural abnormalities- X-ray of right hand: Temporary calcified zone at distal end of metacarpals, short and broad metacarpals and phalangesOther Tests:- 24-h ambulatory EEG: Abnormal electrical activity in brain- Genetic testing: Compound heterozygous variants in WDR19 gene (c.98+1G>C and c.3533G>A) and TG gene (c.274+2T>G and c.4889A>G)",
